- Relationship with the newborn: A good or very good relationship likely lowers risk of postpartum depression; neutral or bad relationships may increase risk.
- Pregnancy plan: Planned pregnancies are generally associated with lower risk; unplanned pregnancies may increase risk.
- Major changes or losses during pregnancy: Experiencing major changes or losses tends to increase risk of postpartum depression.
- Relationship with the in-laws: Good or friendly relationships may reduce stress and risk; bad or poor relationships may increase risk.
- Age of newborn: Risk of postpartum depression may vary with newborn age; early months may have higher risk due to adjustment period.
- Education Level: Higher education may be protective due to better coping mechanisms and resources; lower education may increase risk.
- Birth compliancy: Complications during birth may increase stress and risk of postpartum depression.
- Gender of newborn: Generally less influential, but cultural or personal preferences might affect risk.
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy: Higher income may reduce stress and risk; lower income may increase risk.
- Occupation After Your Latest Childbirth: Occupations with more social support or engagement may reduce risk; housewife or unemployed status may increase risk due to isolation.

